<div class="wrap">

<header class="mast">
  <p class="eyebrow">Final design — five spikes, no schema changes</p>
  <h1>Prodma on Lobu</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Three things I proposed last round are gone — the version column, the audit opt-in, and
    row-level read scope. But asking whether the design was complete turned up two things nothing
    in Lobu can do yet, and one of them is load-bearing for an ERP.
  </p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span><b>Source</b> lobu@main · prodma 637k live LOC</span>
    <span><b>Method</b> claim → grep → file:line</span>
    <span><b>DB changes</b> none</span>
    <span><b>Spikes</b> 5</span>
  </div>
</header>

<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Section 01</p>
  <h2>Where this landed</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Every round of this analysis has ended the same way: something I called a gap turned out to
      be a primitive I hadn't found, or a decision the repo had already made and written down. This
      round that happened three more times, and one of them reversed an answer I gave two days ago.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pattern is worth naming, because it's the actual finding. Lobu is not missing pieces for
      this problem. It's that the pieces are at a different granularity than an ERP developer
      expects — field-level where you'd expect row-level, data where you'd expect code, declarative
      where you'd expect a table. Almost every gap I've reported has dissolved once I found the
      right grain.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="verdictbox">
    <p><strong>What changed since the last version of this document:</strong></p>
    <ul class="tight" style="margin-top:12px;margin-bottom:0">
      <li><strong><code>xmin</code> for concurrency</strong> — withdrawn. It's the wrong grain, and the real bug is in the browser, not the database.</li>
      <li><strong><code>x-durable-audit</code> opt-in</strong> — withdrawn. The flag protects against a cost that doesn't exist.</li>
      <li><strong>Row-level read scope</strong> — closed, not deferred. The customer answered it.</li>
      <li><strong>Materialized views</strong> — explained below, and the honest answer is that this codebase has never used one and probably shouldn't start.</li>
      <li><strong>Two genuine gaps found</strong> — a transition can refuse a write but cannot cause one, and there's no way to allocate a document number. Section 06.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Section 02</p>
  <h2>Your four questions</h2>

  <div class="claims">

    <div class="claim">
      <span class="id">Q — CONCURRENCY</span>
      <h3>Is using <code>xmin</code> the best idea?</h3>
      <p class="said">"is using xmin the best idea?"</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>No, and following the question found a better answer</dt>
        <dd>
          I went looking for what <code>xmin</code> would actually protect and discovered the server
          doesn't have the bug I was fixing. <code>updateEntity</code> reads the row under
          <code>FOR UPDATE</code> <em>inside</em> the transaction, then merges the incoming fields
          into that freshly-read copy, key by key. Two people editing different fields of the same
          invoice both land correctly today. There is no lost update at the field level.
        </dd>
        <dt>Where the real bug is</dt>
        <dd>
          In the browser. The entity edit form computes one dirty flag for the whole metadata object
          and then sends <em>everything</em> — <code>metadata: metadataDirty ? metadata : undefined</code>.
          So when two people have the same invoice open and one changes the date while the other
          changes a price, both saves carry all thirty fields, including each person's stale copy of
          what the other just changed. The server's per-field merge does exactly what it's told and
          the second save silently reverts the first.
        </dd>
        <dt>Why <code>xmin</code> is the wrong shape</dt>
        <dd>
          It's a <em>row</em> token on a system whose concurrency model is <em>field</em>-grained. It
          would reject the exact edits the merge already handles correctly — two people working on
          different parts of one document, which in an ERP is the normal case, not the exception. It
          would trade a rare silent overwrite for a frequent spurious conflict, which users learn to
          click through, which is worse than either.
        </dd>
        <dt>The right fix</dt>
        <dd>
          Send only the keys that changed. One file in the frontend, no server change, no schema
          change, and it makes the existing merge do what it was designed to do. Same-field
          simultaneous edits stay last-write-wins — and if we ever want to catch those,
          <code>field_controls</code> already stamps <code>set_at</code> per field, which is a
          conflict token at the correct grain and it's already being written.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <div class="verdict"><b>Withdrawn.</b> Not a database problem. Fix the client payload; the server is already right.</div>
      <p class="src">
        <span>packages/server/src/utils/entity-management.ts:616 — FOR UPDATE, then :728 computeFieldMerge over the locked read</span>
        <span>packages/owletto/src/components/entity-tabs/index.tsx:105 — whole-object dirty flag</span>
        <span>packages/server/src/utils/entity-field-merge.ts:22 — FieldControl { note, set_by, set_at }</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="claim">
      <span class="id">Q — AUDIT</span>
      <h3><code>x-durable-audit</code> isn't enabled all the time — do we need it?</h3>
      <p class="said">"x-durable-audit isn't all enabled all the time, do we need this"</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>No. Drop the flag</dt>
        <dd>
          I added it because making the audit write durable everywhere felt like a platform-wide
          change to failure semantics, and those deserve an opt-in. But that reasoning assumed the
          audit write was <em>new</em>. It isn't. Every entity update already writes a change event
          today — <code>recordChangeEvent</code> at <code>manage_entity.ts:547</code>, unconditional,
          on every single update.
        </dd>
        <dt>So what actually changes</dt>
        <dd>
          Nothing about volume. The same row gets written either way. The only difference is
          <em>when</em>: today it happens after the transaction commits, with one retry, and if that
          fails it logs an error and drops the record. Moving it inside the transaction that's
          already open means the change and its record land together or not at all.
        </dd>
        <dt>What a flag would actually be protecting</dt>
        <dd>
          A per-type flag would mean some entity types have a trustworthy history and others quietly
          don't — and nobody looking at a history view would know which kind they were looking at.
          That's a worse property than either option on its own. If durable-everywhere turns out to
          cost something real, that's a finding worth having; a flag would just hide it.
        </dd>
        <dt>One thing that stays true</dt>
        <dd>
          The audit is still wrong in a way the flag never addressed: the before-snapshot is read at
          <code>manage_entity.ts:421</code>, before the row lock is taken. Under two concurrent
          writers the recorded "old value" can be someone else's. That's a correctness bug and it
          gets fixed regardless.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <div class="verdict"><b>Withdrawn.</b> Audit commits with the row, for every type, no opt-in.</div>
      <p class="src">
        <span>packages/server/src/tools/admin/manage_entity.ts:421 (snapshot) vs :547 (record)</span>
        <span>packages/server/src/utils/entity-management.ts:616 — the lock, taken after the snapshot</span>
        <span>packages/server/src/utils/insert-event.ts:386 — insertEvent already accepts a tx handle</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="claim">
      <span class="id">Q — LINKS</span>
      <h3>Do we need <code>x-link-entity-type</code>, what problem does it solve?</h3>
      <p class="said">"do we need this x-link-entity-type what problem does it solve"</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>The problem</dt>
        <dd>
          An invoice stores <code>customer_id: 42</code>. On its own that's just a number. The table
          view shows "42". The agent can't tell whether it's a customer, a product, or a quantity. A
          picker can't exist because nothing knows what to offer. JSON Schema has no concept of a
          foreign key, so the reference is invisible to every consumer.
        </dd>
        <dt>What the keyword does</dt>
        <dd>
          It declares the target type. The server then resolves the id into
          <code>{slug, name}</code> before the row leaves, so the UI renders "Acme Ltd" instead of
          "42" without a second round trip, and any consumer — table, form, agent, export — knows
          what the number points at. It's a foreign key declaration for a schemaless column.
        </dd>
        <dt>Why not a relationship row instead</dt>
        <dd>
          That's the real alternative, and for some links it's the better one.
          <code>entity_relationships</code> gives you a traversable graph — "every document touching
          this customer" — at the cost of a second row and a second write outside the document. For
          an ERP document the id-in-metadata is right: the customer reference is <em>part of</em> the
          invoice, it must be written atomically with it, and it must freeze with it. A relationship
          row could be edited after the invoice is posted, which is exactly what the freeze exists to
          prevent.
        </dd>
        <dt>Verdict</dt>
        <dd>
          Keep it, and note that it's the proof the whole rule design works. It's an unrecognised
          keyword living in <code>metadata_schema</code>, honored independently by the server and by
          Owletto, with no column and no deploy. Everything I'm proposing for transition rules is the
          same move on a keyword that already ships.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <div class="verdict"><b>Keep.</b> It's a foreign key for a schemaless field — and the precedent the rule design rests on.</div>
      <p class="src">
        <span>packages/server/src/tools/admin/manage_entity.ts:1146 — server-side resolution to {slug, name}</span>
        <span>packages/owletto/src/components/entities/entity-table-view.tsx:201 — independent UI consumer</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="claim">
      <span class="id">Q — METRICS</span>
      <h3>The doc might be old — what's a materialized view, and do we need one?</h3>
      <p class="said">
        "the design doc might be old for metrics, what's materialized-view and do we have it or do
        we still need it with our today's metrics design change"
      </p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Is the doc old</dt>
        <dd>
          No — and I checked rather than assumed, because you were right to suspect it. The metric
          compiler last changed substantively on 2026-06-16; everything touching that directory since
          is incidental. The doc describes the code as it stands. It's a year-zero design that hasn't
          been revisited, which is different from being stale.
        </dd>
        <dt>What a materialized view is</dt>
        <dd>
          A normal SQL view is a saved query — every read re-runs it against live data, always
          correct, and it gets slower as the data grows. A <em>materialized</em> view stores the
          result as a real table on disk. Reads are instant because they're just a table scan, but
          the answer is as old as the last refresh, and something has to run that refresh on a
          schedule. You trade freshness for speed.
        </dd>
        <dt>Do we have one</dt>
        <dd>
          No. Zero across the entire migration history and the entire server. Derived entity types
          are plain views — the query runs on every read. The refresh machinery a materialized view
          would need doesn't exist either.
        </dd>
        <dt>Do we need one — the honest answer</dt>
        <dd>
          Probably not, and possibly never. The design doc names materialized views as the escape
          hatch for a slow aggregate, but that instruction has sat unused for two months while the
          codebase repeatedly solved the same problem a different way: materializing a
          <em>column</em> at write time. Behaviors carry a completion stamp; MCP activity carries a
          resolved identity. When Lobu has needed to make a read fast, it has never once reached for
          a materialized view.
        </dd>
        <dt>Which leaves one measurement</dt>
        <dd>
          Start with the plain derived view, because it's always correct and costs nothing to build.
          If it's fast enough at Prodma's volume — and at their size it very likely is — the whole
          question was theoretical. If it isn't, the choice is between a refresh schedule this
          codebase has never built and a write-time column it has built repeatedly, and the second
          one wins on precedent alone.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <div class="verdict"><b>No materialized view.</b> Plain derived view, measure, and only then decide — with the codebase's own habit as the tiebreak.</div>
      <p class="src">
        <span>docs/plans/define-metric-design.md:41, :494 — the ops-toggle instruction</span>
        <span>packages/server/src/metrics/compiler.ts — last substantive change 2026-06-16</span>
        <span>0 occurrences of CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW in packages/ or db/</span>
        <span>packages/server/src/tools/admin/manage_behaviors/list.ts:59 · utils/table-schema.ts:510 — the write-time-column habit</span>
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Section 03</p>
  <h2>What the customer's answer settles</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Two things came back from Prodma's owner, and between them they close the largest open
      question and reshape the most uncertain spike.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cmp">
    <div>
      <h3>"Chat plus CRUD pages is enough"</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>No bespoke screens. 441 forms do not become 441 components</li>
        <li>The generic entity pages <em>are</em> the product, not a fallback</li>
        <li>Which raises the stakes on them, rather than lowering them</li>
        <li>A document whose lines can't be displayed has no working page at all</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>"Operate based on what you know" — permissions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Prodma hides fields client-side, with <code>.Visible = false</code> in the form</li>
        <li>That's per-screen and per-field, applied in the UI — not row filtering</li>
        <li>So Lobu's existing tool-access tier covers the shape they actually have</li>
        <li>Row-level read scope is closed, not deferred</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>The assumption, stated plainly so it can be falsified.</strong> I'm reading Prodma's
      permission model from how the WinForms code hides things — client-side field visibility, no
      row predicate anywhere I looked. That's consistent with everything I've seen, but the
      truncated permission sheets are the evidence I'd have used to confirm it, and I'm proceeding
      without them by decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tell that I'm wrong is specific and will appear in the first demo: if the owner looks at
      the customer list and asks why one salesperson can see another's accounts, the model is
      row-level, and the deferred design comes back. Worth saying out loud before the demo rather
      than discovering it live.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Section 04</p>
  <h2>The design</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Three layers. No new table, no new column, no new primitive. Two of the three are moving an
      existing call to a better place.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="cmp">
    <div>
      <h3>1 · Rules are data</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>An <code>x-transitions</code> block on the type's <code>metadata_schema</code></li>
        <li>Written by an agent at runtime, no deploy — same mechanism as <code>x-link-entity-type</code></li>
        <li>Names the state field, the legal moves, the point past which nothing changes</li>
        <li>Schema updates are approval-queued, so a rule can't be quietly removed</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>2 · Enforcement is deterministic</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>One new file registering a <code>MutationInterceptor</code></li>
        <li>Reads the keyword, compares current to proposed, allows or denies</li>
        <li>Denies regardless of who asks — no principal short-circuit</li>
        <li>No model in the write path: same input, same answer, every time</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>3 · History commits with the row</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Before-snapshot taken from the locked row, not an earlier separate read</li>
        <li>Change event written with the transaction handle it already accepts</li>
        <li>Every type, no opt-in — it's the same write, moved</li>
        <li>Extended to create, delete, link and merge, which record nothing today</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note warn">
    <p>
      <strong>Two things this must not do,</strong> both found in review and both easy to get wrong
      on the way in.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Don't open the human short-circuit.</strong> The policy resolver returns
      <em>allow</em> immediately for human principals, and downstream of it sits a branch that makes
      any human-owned field require approval unconditionally. That branch is only correct
      <em>because</em> the caller is never human. Remove the short-circuit and every person editing a
      field they themselves last touched gets an approval card addressed to themselves. The fix is to
      route human updates <em>into</em> the gate — today <code>entity-management.ts:654</code> skips
      the whole call for them — while leaving the policy resolver untouched. The new interceptor
      decides on its own terms; the existing one keeps saying allow.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Don't forget the second door.</strong> <code>applyEntityFieldChangeProposal</code>
      writes through <code>mergeEntityFields</code> with no gate call anywhere in the file. A change
      proposed while an invoice was a draft, and approved after it was posted, walks straight into the
      frozen document. The freeze has to be checked where the write happens, not only where the
      request arrives.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Section 05</p>
  <h2>Five spikes</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Each is a question with a way to come back "no". A spike that can only succeed isn't a spike,
      it's a task — and the point is to find where Lobu doesn't hold, not to demonstrate that it
      does.
    </p>
